  [OK] L2a the 3-parameter family provably NESTS the 2-parameter one (L0a) and its floor is therefore no higher: 73.68 vs 75.61 canonical, 77.76 vs 79.88 alt. This tests the SEARCH, not the physics -- if the 3-parameter scan had come out above the 2-parameter one it would mean the optimiser had missed the diagonal and every floor below would be unusable
  [OK] L2b THE THIRD PARAMETER BUYS 1.94 Msun/pc^2 canonical (75.61 -> 73.68) and 2.12 alt (79.88 -> 77.76) -- enough to take the CANONICAL floor just below the highest published central of 74 (73.68, i.e. 0.32 inside it) but NOT the alt footing (77.76), and neither footing comes near Bovy & Rix's 68: +1.42 / +2.44 sigma on their own +-4. The thick disc does not rescue it, and the reason is arithmetic: at z_d = 900 pc the thick disc still puts 71% of its column INSIDE |z| < 1.1 kpc, so 'mass at height' is only a 29% escape per unit mass moved. The winning cell also pins Sigma_* at its -2 sigma edge and misses v_c by 11 sigma

======================================================================================================================
  L3  (A) THE 4-PARAMETER FLOOR -- adding the thin-disc SCALE HEIGHT z_d
======================================================================================================================
  z_d is close to a pure knob on the binding constraint: the total stellar mass is INDEPENDENT of it,
  while the fraction of the thin disc inside |z| < 1.1 kpc is 1 - exp(-1.1/z_d) -- 97.4% at the published 300 pc,
  70.5% at 900 pc. The family the lane specifies carries NO prior on z_d, which is exactly what has
  to be priced. Both directions are scanned (z_d = 210-900 pc).

  4p FLOOR, footing canon: 71.92 Msun/pc^2   (3p 73.68, 2p 75.61)   z_d wanted = 924 pc = +12.5 sigma vs 300 +- 50
