  4p floor with z_d held inside 2 sigma of 300 +- 50 pc, footing canon: 73.62 (z_d = 309 pc)
  4p floor with z_d held inside 2 sigma of 300 +- 50 pc, footing alt: 77.71 (z_d = 309 pc)
  [OK] L3a the 4-parameter family nests the 3-parameter one and the floor is monotone: 71.92 <= 73.68 <= 75.61 canonical, 75.90 <= 77.76 <= 79.88 alt. And restricting z_d to within 2 sigma of the published 300 +- 50 pc RAISES it back to 73.62 / 77.71 -- which is the honest price tag on the fourth parameter: essentially all of what it buys, it buys with a scale height the star counts exclude
  [OK] L3b *** THE FOURTH PARAMETER: the floor reaches 71.92 canonical / 75.90 alt. So the CANONICAL floor does come inside the published range (<= 74) at 3 and 4 parameters, the ALT footing never does, and NEITHER reaches Bovy & Rix's 68 -- the number that decides this front -- at any parameter count (+0.98 / +1.97 sigma on their +-4). And the canonical reduction from 73.68 to 71.92 is bought at z_d = 924 pc, +12 sigma from the published 300 +- 50 pc (Bland-Hawthorn & Gerhard 2016) -- a thin disc as thick as the THICK disc. Held to a defensible z_d the 4-parameter floor is 73.62 / 77.71, i.e. it collapses back onto the 3-parameter value. *** So on this lane's question: extra baryon freedom moves the floor by 2.0-3.7 Msun/pc^2, enough to matter against 74 on one footing and not enough to matter against 68 on either. The over-delivery relative to Bovy & Rix is NOT a baryon-model artefact ***

