  [OK] L4a *** THE DECIDING CONTROL, AND IT SPLITS. *** (i) alpha=2 does NOT clear at any parameter count on either footing: prior-restricted 5.073 (2p) -> 4.915 (3p/4p best), unrestricted 2.955 -> 2.816, all far above 2. So the extra parameters do NOT hand the box to the kernel this corpus retired, and against THAT comparator the extension is physics rather than freedom. (ii) But the literature's SIMPLE mu, which fails at two parameters (2.018 -- and note that the committed script's published 2.304 for it is limited to that script's search BOX; on the domain-wide prior-restricted scan simple mu is already at 2.018 with two parameters), CLEARS as soon as a third is added: 2.018 -> 1.876 -> 1.554. *** So the box test keeps its power against alpha=2 and LOSES it against the literature kernel: at three or more baryon parameters, 'Route A clears and the comparator does not' is true only of alpha=2, and Route A's clearance no longer distinguishes it from standard simple-mu MOND. *** The check asserts both halves -- that the extension HELPS alpha=2 (it must, the families nest) and that it does not help it enough to clear
  [OK] L4b and the ORDERING of the kernels is preserved at every parameter count: Route A's best worst-constraint stays below alpha=2's at 2, 3 and 4 parameters (1.489/5.073, 1.486/4.915, 1.423/4.915). The extension does not INVERT the comparison; what L4a settles is only whether the test still SEPARATES them
  [OK] L4c the restricted minimax is MONOTONE in the parameter count for all five kernel x footing combinations, as nesting requires -- the 4-parameter scan is seeded with every 3-parameter optimum and the 3-parameter scan with every 2-parameter one, so a non-monotone entry would be a visible search failure rather than a result. (An unseeded first pass of this script DID produce one, alpha=2 reading 6.418 at 4p against 6.216 at 3p, purely because the coarser 4-parameter grid missed the 3-parameter optimum; the seeding is what removes it)

